          %h3#in_type_theory In type theory
          %p
            There are multiple ways to define the flat modality in type theory, by using
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            and unjustified inference rules, and by using
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+deduction"} natural deduction
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inference+rules"} inference rules
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+deduction"} natural deduction
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inference+rules"} inference rules
                for flat modalities are simple; they involve the usual 4 rules for positive types:
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formation+rules"} formation rules
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/introduction+rules"} introduction rules
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elimination+rules"} elimination rules
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computation+rules"} computation rules
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo :
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:A
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo :
                        %mo :
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x::A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
                such as
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+contexts"} split contexts
            %li
              %p
                When using
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
                or unjustified inference rules, the flat modality could be defined in vanilla
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sharp+modality"} sharp modality
                and a
